<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cochabamba Water War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1999">1999</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cochabamba Water War was a series of protests in the Bolivian city of Cochabamba from December 1999 to April 2000 against the privatisation of the municipal water supply. The concession had been given to Aguas del Tunari, a consortium led by the US company Bechtel, and water prices rose sharply. After strikes, roadblocks and a protester's death, the government cancelled the contract.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After hyperinflation reached an annual rate of 25000% in 1985, successive Bolivian governments followed the World Bank's provisions to qualify for loans, and a condition of one loan was to privatise the water utilities of La Paz and Cochabamba. Aguas del Tunari was the only bidder for SEMAPA, the city's state water agency, and the government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-banzer">Hugo Banzer</a> signed a 40-year concession guaranteeing the consortium a minimum annual return of 15%. The contract bound it to finish the stalled Misicuni dam, a project backed by the mayor, Manfred Reyes Villa. Law 2029, passed to secure the deal, led many to fear that communal and irrigation water would also be taken over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The company raised rates by an average of 35% to about 20 dollars a month, when many customers earned about 100 dollars a month. Irrigators of the Federación Departamental Cochabambina de Regantes under Omar Fernandez and factory workers' unions under Oscar Olivera formed the Coordinadora, which led protests from January 2000. A general strike shut the city for four days, and clashes on 4 February left 70 protesters and 51 policemen injured. In March the Coordinadora held its own referendum, in which 96% of fifty thousand votes demanded the contract be cancelled.</p><p>In April protesters took the central plaza again, the Coordinadora's leaders were arrested, and roadblocks spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Banzer declared a state of siege on 8 April, suspending some constitutional guarantees and curtailing the press. After an army captain, Robinson Iriarte de la Fuente, was filmed firing into a crowd and killing the student Víctor Hugo Daza, the consortium's executives fled to Santa Cruz. The government declared the contract revoked, and Law 2029 was changed in a special session on 11 April 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Aguas del Tunari filed a claim at the International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes; in a settlement of 19 January 2006 its shareholders withdrew their claims. A military tribunal acquitted Iriarte in March 2002. Water prices returned to their earlier levels under a restored SEMAPA, but as late as 2005 half of the city's 600,000 people had no water connection. The struggle raised the profile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evo-morales">Evo Morales</a>, elected president in 2005, and won anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a> groups' support; it was later drawn on by films including Even the Rain and Quantum of Solace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cochabamba Water War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1990s Gonzalo Sánchez de Lozada privatised state companies and decentralised government, but protests against foreign control of resources, notably the <b>Cochabamba Water War</b> of 2000 and the Gas War of 2003, forced him out.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
